---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Signup from '../components/Signup.astro';

// Answers about Pennsylvania describe HB 504 as passed by the State House (PN 1628).
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Would I still get my electricity from my current utility?',
    a: "Yes. Your utility still delivers your power, sends your bill, and handles outages and repairs. Community solar adds a credit to that bill. It doesn't replace your utility.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Would community solar keep my lights on during a power outage?',
    a: "No. The solar project feeds the grid, not your home, so it isn't backup power. If your neighborhood loses power, your home does too, just as it does today.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Would it cost anything to join?',
    a: "Under HB 504 as passed by the State House, subscriptions couldn't include any upfront or sign-on fees or credit checks, and nothing gets installed at your home.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How much would I save?',
    a: "The bill requires the cost of a subscription to be less than the value of the bill credit it earns, so subscribers come out ahead. The exact savings would depend on rules the Pennsylvania Public Utility Commission writes and the terms each project offers. Other states guarantee minimum savings, from 5% in New York to 15% in New Jersey and 20% in Massachusetts. Your credit would also change month to month, since solar projects produce more in summer than in winter.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Would I get two bills?',
    a: "No. The bill calls for net crediting: your utility would put the subscription fee and the solar credit on your regular monthly bill and show you the difference. Your utility would keep billing for everything else, just as it does now.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I cancel? Are there cancellation fees?',
    a: "You could leave a subscription. The bill does not allow termination or cancellation fees.",
  },
  {
    q: 'What if I miss a subscription payment?',
    a: "You could lose your subscription, but the bill says missing a subscription payment can't affect your electric service.",
  },
  {
    q: 'What if I move?',
    a: "You could take your subscription with you if you move within the same utility's service area. A move to an area served by a different utility would mean starting a new subscription there.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Would low-income households be included?',
    a: "Yes. The bill directs the Public Utility Commission to increase participation by low-income households (at or below 150% of the federal poverty line) and to use available federal money to give income-qualified households larger guaranteed savings.",
  },
  {
    q: 'How would I know what I am signing up for?',
    a: "The Public Utility Commission would write consumer protection rules and create a standard disclosure form, in English and Spanish, that companies would have to give you before you subscribe. It would cover future costs and benefits and your rights.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I sign up today?',
    a: "Not yet. Pennsylvania has no community solar program until the State Senate passes the bill and the Governor signs it. Join our list and we'll email you when that changes.",
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
};
---
<Base
  title="What is community solar?"
  description="A plain-English guide to community solar: how shared solar projects work, who can join, how bill credits work, and what it could mean for Pennsylvania."
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <div class="eyebrow">The basics</div>
      <h1>What is community solar?</h1>
      <p>
        Community solar is a way for many households to share one solar project. Instead of putting
        panels on your own roof, you subscribe to a share of a solar array built somewhere nearby.
        The electricity it produces goes onto the local grid, and you get credit for your share
        on your electric bill.
      </p>

      <h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <p>About half of U.S. households can't put solar on their own homes. Community solar was designed for them:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Renters</strong> who don't own their roof</li>
        <li><strong>Apartment and condo residents</strong> in shared buildings</li>
        <li><strong>Homeowners</strong> whose roofs are shaded, too small, or facing the wrong way</li>
        <li><strong>Households that can't afford</strong> the upfront cost of rooftop panels</li>
        <li><strong>Small businesses, schools, and nonprofits</strong> that lease their space</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>A developer builds a solar project</strong> in your utility's service area.</li>
        <li><strong>You subscribe to a share</strong> of the project's output. Nothing is installed at your home.</li>
        <li><strong>The project sends power to the grid</strong>, and your utility tracks your share.</li>
        <li><strong>You receive bill credits</strong> for your share of the energy, usually on the same utility bill you get today.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What community solar is not</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>It's not rooftop solar.</strong> Nothing gets installed at your home.</li>
        <li><strong>It doesn't change your utility.</strong> Your local utility still delivers your power and handles outages.</li>
        <li><strong>It's not the same as choosing an electric supplier.</strong> Pennsylvania already lets you shop for a supplier. Community solar is a separate program built around bill credits from a local project.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where it already exists</h2>
      <p>
        About two dozen states and Washington, D.C. have community solar programs, including our
        neighbors New Jersey, New York, and Maryland. Each state sets its own rules for who can
        join, how credits are calculated, and how much of each project is reserved for
        lower-income households.
        <a href="/why-community-solar">See how the leading states compare →</a>
      </p>

      <h2>What about Pennsylvania?</h2>
      <p>
        Pennsylvania doesn't yet have a law that lets community solar projects serve households
        across the state. A bill to create that program has passed the State House and is
        waiting on the State Senate.
        <a href="/pennsylvania">Read where it stands →</a>
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Common questions</h2>
      <p class="note">Answers about Pennsylvania describe <a href="/pennsylvania">HB 504</a> as passed by the State House. Details could change before it becomes law.</p>
      <div class="faq">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>A note on savings:</strong> how much subscribers save, and how sign-up works, will depend
        on the rules Pennsylvania adopts. We'll explain the details here once they're known.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Signup />
</Base>

<style>
  .faq details { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 12px 0; }
  .faq summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; color: var(--green-900); }
  .faq details p { margin: 10px 0 0; }
  .note { font-size: .9rem; color: var(--muted); }
</style>
